msgid "This part is not covered in class. It is for reading at home: a simulation of the portfolio loss with NumPy - the \"modelling\" side of the team's work - and a short note on SAS files."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:999
msgid "Monte Carlo: the distribution of the portfolio loss"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1001
msgid "The expected loss (`PD x LGD x EAD`, summed) is one number. A risk manager also wants the **distribution** of the loss: how bad can a year be? A simple simulation:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1004
msgid "For each loan, draw a random number; the loan defaults if it is below its PD."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1005
msgid "The loss of the year is the sum of `LGD x EAD` of the defaulted loans."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1006
msgid "Repeat 10,000 times; look at the distribution of the 10,000 losses."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1008
msgid "NumPy does all scenarios at once - a 10,000 x 400 array of random numbers:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1034
msgid "The mean of the simulation is close to the expected loss of homework 4 (1,140,106.56) - the formula is the mean of this distribution. The 99.9% quantile is far higher: one or two large project finance loans that default together make a bad year. (This simple model treats the defaults as independent; real credit portfolio models add a common factor for the economy.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1040
msgid "A histogram (charts: session 16):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1062
msgid "**Exercises**"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1064
msgid "Run the simulation with LGD 0.25 and 0.60. How do the mean and the 99.9% quantile change? Are they proportional to the LGD?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1066
msgid "Remove the 7 loans over EUR 5 million (`ead > 5e6`) and run it again. What happens to the 99.9% quantile - and why is that the concentration risk of homework 5?"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1069 site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1104
msgid "Answer"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1070
msgid "Both are proportional: the losses are `defaults x LGD x EAD`, so all of them scale with the LGD (with the same seed, exactly)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1072
msgid "`keep = ead <= 5e6`, then use `pd_loan[keep]` and `ead[keep]`. The mean drops a little, the 99.9% quantile drops a lot: the tail of the distribution comes from the few very large loans - the concentration of the top 10 (47% of the portfolio, homework 5)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1078
msgid "Reading SAS datasets"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1080
msgid "`pd.read_sas` reads `.sas7bdat` (and `.xpt`) files without SAS. Points to know:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1082
msgid "**Encoding:** give `encoding=` (the SAS session encoding, often `latin-1` or `utf-8`); without it, text columns come as bytes (`b'...'`)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1084
msgid "**Dates:** SAS date variables with a date format arrive as `datetime64`; without a format, as numbers of days since 1960-01-01 - convert with `pd.to_datetime(x, unit=\"D\", origin=\"1960-01-01\")`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1087
msgid "**Missing values:** `.` and the special missing values `.A` - `.Z` become `NaN`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1088
msgid "**Big files:** `chunksize=` gives pieces (session 13, Self-study)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1089
msgid "**Labels and formats:** `pyreadstat.read_sas7bdat(path)` returns `(df, meta)`; `meta.column_names_to_labels` maps the variable names to their labels."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1099
msgid "**Exercise**"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1101
msgid "Convert the SAS date numbers `0`, `366` and `24379` into dates. What is the SAS date of 30.09.2026?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/14-numpy-pandas-intro.md:1110
msgid "`0` is 1960-01-01, `366` is 1961-01-01 (1960 was a leap year), `24379` is 2026-09-30."
msgstr ""

